open_gaps:
Suggested pilot for the orchestrator (not run here, and within about 6 GPU-hours): on wrwl/ppo.py with SharedAgent, train 3–4 ManiSkill tasks with binary reward, 2–3 seeds per arm. Arms:
- plain multi-task PPO;
- plus per-task value normalisation or a multi-head critic;
- a shared actor with separate per-task critics;
- critic-free Monte Carlo advantages (GRPO-style);
- separate actors with a shared critic (upper bound for actor conflict);
- a 2x-width actor.
Also log the cosine between actor gradients on cross-task latent-neighbour pairs against within-task neighbour pairs. If per-task critics or the wider network close most of the gap, the premise for setting (a) fails and we should drop it.

how_it_differs:
Our own WRWL/G3' anchor KL-anchored the mean under a gate built from value-based branch labels. Those labels measured detour cost and therefore anchored the release region, and the policy could release only once lambda had annealed to about 0. CoFork has no anchor and no labels:
- outside open cells the copy is exact by construction;
- inside them PPO is free at once;
- the gate comes from the agent's own reward signal after it appears, consistent with G2' showing that cold-start prediction fails;
- opening a fork costs nothing, because it starts as a clone.

PEX, Dodeja et al. and PARTS pair a frozen base with a new policy or residual chosen per state by different means: a target-task Q (PEX), base uncertainty with a decaying threshold (Dodeja et al.), or human-identified bottleneck subtasks with agent-written selectors (PARTS). CoFork differs in five ways:
- it decides where to learn with a critic-free conflict test;
- the new branch starts as a clone, not a random residual;
- it runs on-policy with PPO;
- it forks at the representation level, sharing the frozen lower layers;
- it adds fork-local noise.

pilot.metric:
Primary: steps to eval success_once >= 0.8 (median over seeds, right-censored at the cap; on Stack also the Kaplan-Meier fraction reaching 0.8 and RMST), plus normalised success AUC on [0, cap].
Mechanism logs:
- gated fraction over time;
- share of gated mass by region (TCP-cube distance > 5 cm vs contact on push; conflict_probe's six phases on Stack);
- number of cells opened before the first rewarded episode (false positives);
- first-update KL;
- deterministic lift rate at 2.5M-step checkpoints before the first success (erosion).

pilot.kill:
Drop the claim that conflict localisation matters if, in every setting where locality is possible (gated fraction <= 0.5), CoFork-random is within 15% of CoFork's median steps-to-80%.
Drop CoFork altogether if any of these holds:
- CoFork-all is within 15% of CoFork on both T45 settings, meaning the gain is just a frozen lower trunk;
- ft-reset is within 15% on all three push settings, meaning the gain was the std reset;
- CoFork is at least 1.5x slower than ft-reset on T45 sparse (detection latency);
- cells open before any reward in 2 or more sparse runs.
On Stack, if CoFork does not beat frozen-readout in the number of seeds reaching 0.8, structure adds nothing beyond freezing, and the Stack problem is exploration only.
If CoFork-noise0 loses most of CoFork's gain, report the result as localised exploration, not as a structural effect.

